WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.940 --> 00:00:06.370
Despite the popularity of Apple’s iconic
designs, the creative genius behind them remains

00:00:06.370 --> 00:00:07.859
relatively unknown.

00:00:07.859 --> 00:00:13.030
I’d like to ask Jony Ive, our SVP of design,
to come up and take us through that.

00:00:13.030 --> 00:00:15.570
Without Jony Ive, Apple wouldn’t be Apple.

00:00:15.570 --> 00:00:16.630
Good morning.

00:00:16.630 --> 00:00:20.470
He created products that are not only functional
but emotional.

00:00:20.470 --> 00:00:25.490
He made people feel something when they held
an iPhone in their hand.

00:00:25.490 --> 00:00:26.789
Design was in his blood.

00:00:26.789 --> 00:00:31.890
His father was a silversmith and teacher who
helped craft a new design curriculum for UK

00:00:31.890 --> 00:00:34.520
schools from which his son benefitted.

00:00:34.520 --> 00:00:37.750
Mike Ive constantly talked to his son about
design.

00:00:37.750 --> 00:00:42.300
Leander Kahney’s book on Jony Ive describes
how, when they walked down the street, Mike

00:00:42.300 --> 00:00:46.530
would point out the different types of streetlamps
to his son and how their shape affected how

00:00:46.530 --> 00:00:48.620
the light was cast.

00:00:48.620 --> 00:00:52.960
Design offered him an outlet for his creativity,
which is a common trait among people like

00:00:52.960 --> 00:00:54.910
him who have dyslexia.

00:00:54.910 --> 00:00:59.710
As his Christmas gift, his father would give
him full access to his workshop, where he

00:00:59.710 --> 00:01:03.910
could build anything his heart desired so
long as he sketched it first.

00:01:03.910 --> 00:01:08.600
Jony told Steve Job’s biographer Walter
Isaacson that, “I always understood the

00:01:08.600 --> 00:01:10.570
beauty of things made by hand.

00:01:10.570 --> 00:01:15.790
I came to realize that what was really important
was the care that was put into it.”

00:01:15.790 --> 00:01:20.659
By the age of 13, he knew he wanted to be
a designer but didn’t know what he wanted

00:01:20.659 --> 00:01:21.920
to design.

00:01:21.920 --> 00:01:26.750
When his work was showcased at a design conference,
the leading London design firm Roberts Weaver

00:01:26.750 --> 00:01:31.790
Group was so impressed that it agreed to support
him through university if Jony promised to

00:01:31.790 --> 00:01:34.380
join the company upon graduation.

00:01:34.380 --> 00:01:40.700
Jony studied in the north of England at Newcastle
Polytechnic, now known as Northumbria University.

00:01:40.700 --> 00:01:45.780
The Bauhaus design philosophy that emerged
in Germany in the early 20th century had made

00:01:45.780 --> 00:01:48.299
its way into British design schools.

00:01:48.299 --> 00:01:53.790
Bauhaus emphasized minimalism: including only
what is needed into designs.

00:01:53.790 --> 00:01:59.119
Objects had clean lines, simple forms, and
were pleasing to the eye.

00:01:59.119 --> 00:02:04.050
That design philosophy heavily influenced
Jony, who brought the Bauhaus aesthetic to

00:02:04.050 --> 00:02:05.050
Apple.

00:02:05.050 --> 00:02:08.550
While at university, Jony interned with the
design firm that was supporting him.

00:02:08.550 --> 00:02:12.940
When he had to design a pen for a Japanese
client, he noticed that people liked to “fiddle”

00:02:12.940 --> 00:02:14.700
around with their pens.

00:02:14.700 --> 00:02:19.420
So he added a ball-and-clip mechanism to the
top of the pen that served no purpose other

00:02:19.420 --> 00:02:21.739
than to give someone something to do.

00:02:21.739 --> 00:02:24.160
This humanized the technology.

00:02:24.160 --> 00:02:27.780
People didn’t want to just write with the
pen – they wanted to touch it and play around

00:02:27.780 --> 00:02:28.780
with it.

00:02:28.780 --> 00:02:31.700
Much like how they “play” with their Apple
products today.

00:02:31.700 --> 00:02:34.099
Jony worked hard as an undergraduate student.

00:02:34.099 --> 00:02:39.300
When a friend visited his apartment, he found
more than a hundred prototypes for a landline

00:02:39.300 --> 00:02:40.680
telephone.

00:02:40.680 --> 00:02:44.150
Creating numerous prototypes became his signature
at Apple.

00:02:44.150 --> 00:02:51.110
Even if the changes were small, he felt they
were important to physically see.

00:02:51.110 --> 00:02:55.420
Although he designed tech products, he didn’t
consider himself a “tech” guy.

00:02:55.420 --> 00:03:07.030
Growing up, the only computer that spoke to
him…was Apple’s Mac.

00:03:07.030 --> 00:03:10.080
He thought it was a lot easier to use than
any other computer.

00:03:10.080 --> 00:03:14.599
And he believed the designers actually cared
about the product and how consumers felt when

00:03:14.599 --> 00:03:15.599
they used it.

00:03:15.599 --> 00:03:16.599
It was a really profound moment, I don’t
think I’ve actually had quite the same sense

00:03:16.599 --> 00:03:17.599
of “wow” with a product before.

00:03:17.599 --> 00:03:20.950
He told the Design Museum in London: “The
more I learnt about this cheeky, almost rebellious

00:03:20.950 --> 00:03:23.819
company the more it appealed to me…”

00:03:23.819 --> 00:03:28.110
The bursaries he received for his stellar
work as a student gave him the chance to travel

00:03:28.110 --> 00:03:32.900
to Silicon Valley, where he visited the studio
of a star designer.

00:03:32.900 --> 00:03:35.989
Robert Brunner was impressed by Jony’s phone
concept.

00:03:35.989 --> 00:03:40.930
Unlike other student projects, it wasn’t
simply a mockup but included the internal

00:03:40.930 --> 00:03:42.040
components.

00:03:42.040 --> 00:03:46.780
Brunner asked him to consider joining his
design firm upon graduation, but Jony had

00:03:46.780 --> 00:03:51.349
already promised to return to London to work
for Roberts Weaver Group, which had been financing

00:03:51.349 --> 00:03:52.670
his education.

00:03:52.670 --> 00:03:58.269
A few months later, Brunner helped Apple establish
its own in-house design studio.

00:03:58.269 --> 00:04:03.239
Up until then, Apple had contracted its design
work out to a German firm.

00:04:03.239 --> 00:04:07.599
After his big trip to America, Jony submitted
a report to the group that awarded him the

00:04:07.599 --> 00:04:13.590
travel bursary, writing: “I immediately
fell in love with San Francisco and desperately

00:04:13.590 --> 00:04:17.239
hope that I can return there sometime in the
future.”

00:04:17.239 --> 00:04:21.570
Back in the UK, he spent a year and a half
with Roberts Weaver Group, before he left

00:04:21.570 --> 00:04:26.550
to join the design startup Tangerine co-founded
by his friend, a former designer at Roberts

00:04:26.550 --> 00:04:27.550
Weaver.

00:04:27.550 --> 00:04:33.169
At Tangerine, the four-person team designed
everything for clients, from TVs to toilets.

00:04:33.169 --> 00:04:38.440
Jony designed a minimalistic comb for a Scottish
hairdresser that went on to win a prestigious

00:04:38.440 --> 00:04:39.440
prize.

00:04:39.440 --> 00:04:43.080
But sometimes, he found it frustrating working
with clients who had a different vision than

00:04:43.080 --> 00:04:44.080
him.

00:04:44.080 --> 00:04:48.770
For example, a major bathroom company was
unhappy with Jony’s toilet designs named

00:04:48.770 --> 00:04:50.650
Raphael, Leonardo, and Donatello.

00:04:50.650 --> 00:04:53.880
They were named after the teenage mutant ninja
turtles.

00:04:53.880 --> 00:04:58.590
The company claimed they were too modern and
too expensive to produce.

00:04:58.590 --> 00:05:04.330
The designs that he put his heart and soul
into were literally flushed down the toilet.

00:05:04.330 --> 00:05:08.039
So when the California designer who had been
impressed by his telephone prototype came

00:05:08.039 --> 00:05:11.130
knocking again, Jony couldn’t resist.

00:05:11.130 --> 00:05:14.150
Brunner was the head of industrial design
at Apple.

00:05:14.150 --> 00:05:17.250
John Sculley was the CEO of Apple at the time.

00:05:17.250 --> 00:05:21.550
Steve Jobs had been forced out of the company
he co-founded and was trying to make his new

00:05:21.550 --> 00:05:26.870
software company NeXT succeed while keeping
his other company Pixar afloat.

00:05:26.870 --> 00:05:31.460
When Brunner hired Tangerine to work on some
potential future products for Apple, the team

00:05:31.460 --> 00:05:36.139
speculated it was just a ploy to try to lure
Jony to California.

00:05:36.139 --> 00:05:38.770
California was a long way from London.

00:05:38.770 --> 00:05:42.460
Jony also wasn’t sure his wife would want
to move so far from home.

00:05:42.460 --> 00:05:45.120
But there were too many reasons to say yes.

00:05:45.120 --> 00:05:50.230
Apple would allow him to focus on his craft
rather than the business side of a startup.

00:05:50.230 --> 00:05:53.139
He could see a design through from start to
finish.

00:05:53.139 --> 00:05:55.790
Plus, he had fallen in love with the Mac.

00:05:55.790 --> 00:05:58.830
So, he packed his bags for the Golden State.

00:05:58.830 --> 00:06:03.949
Jony’s first big assignment at Apple was
the second-generation MessagePad, a personal

00:06:03.949 --> 00:06:07.660
digital assistant that laid the foundation
for the iPhone.

00:06:07.660 --> 00:06:12.430
He designed it to flip open from the top rather
than from the side because then it wasn’t

00:06:12.430 --> 00:06:17.000
culturally specific as people in North America
and Europe would want it to open from left

00:06:17.000 --> 00:06:22.310
to right, whereas people in Japan, for example,
would want it to open from right to left.

00:06:22.310 --> 00:06:25.470
He was always attuned to the needs of the
consumer.

00:06:25.470 --> 00:06:30.340
He won several awards for the MessagePad though
he wasn’t really interested in accolades.

00:06:30.340 --> 00:06:32.740
He was humble and focused on his work.

00:06:32.740 --> 00:06:36.590
Unfortunately, Jony’s stellar design wasn’t
enough.

00:06:36.590 --> 00:06:41.710
The MessagePad bombed due to battery issues
and poor handwriting recognition - which the

00:06:41.710 --> 00:06:43.479
Simpsons made fun of.

00:06:43.479 --> 00:06:48.370
It was a lesson learned, and, in the future,
Jony made sure he could influence the engineering

00:06:48.370 --> 00:06:50.180
process as well.

00:06:50.180 --> 00:06:53.290
Jony quickly emerged as Brunner’s second
in command.

00:06:53.290 --> 00:06:57.490
Brunner wanted his design team to come up
with concepts that would excite people the

00:06:57.490 --> 00:07:00.610
way they’d get excited over a new piece
of furniture.

00:07:00.610 --> 00:07:06.050
At that time, in the early nineties, computers
were becoming increasingly popular in peoples’

00:07:06.050 --> 00:07:07.050
homes.

00:07:07.050 --> 00:07:08.770
But they were big and boxy.

00:07:08.770 --> 00:07:13.900
Jony refined the design of the desktop Mac
into a slim, flat-screen computer.

00:07:13.900 --> 00:07:18.680
The first two units of the Twentieth Anniversary
Mac were given to Apple co-founders Steve

00:07:18.680 --> 00:07:22.139
Jobs and Steve Wozniak in 1997.

00:07:22.139 --> 00:07:26.120
Jobs was brought in as an advisor when Apple
bought his company NeXT.

00:07:26.120 --> 00:07:27.699
But the new Mac failed as well.

00:07:27.699 --> 00:07:30.550
It was very pricey at $9,000.

00:07:30.550 --> 00:07:33.660
Other computers were selling for less than
half that price.

00:07:33.660 --> 00:07:35.940
Brunner felt burnt out and quit.

00:07:35.940 --> 00:07:41.620
So, Jony Ive became the new head of industrial
design at just 29 years old.

00:07:41.620 --> 00:07:43.629
But he had his frustrations, too.

00:07:43.629 --> 00:07:48.380
He felt Apple’s then-CEO, Gilbert Amelio
had no desire to innovate.

00:07:48.380 --> 00:07:51.720
His immediate boss promised him things would
soon be different.

00:07:51.720 --> 00:07:54.650
Amelio was quickly forced out as CEO.

00:07:54.650 --> 00:07:58.400
And Steve Jobs was back at the helm.

00:07:58.400 --> 00:08:01.949
It had been 12 years since Jobs was ousted
from Apple.

00:08:01.949 --> 00:08:05.099
When he returned, he held a meeting of top
executives.

00:08:05.099 --> 00:08:09.800
Ive recalls: “I remember very clearly Steve
announcing that our goal is not just to make

00:08:09.800 --> 00:08:13.069
money but to make great products.”

00:08:13.069 --> 00:08:18.160
Jobs wanted Apple to focus on creating first-class
products that people would be willing to pay

00:08:18.160 --> 00:08:23.539
a premium for, much like the way people are
willing to pay more for a Mercedes than a

00:08:23.539 --> 00:08:24.539
Chevy.

00:08:24.539 --> 00:08:27.470
Jobs had a vision of what he wanted Apple
products to be.

00:08:27.470 --> 00:08:29.170
He told the New York Times:

00:08:29.170 --> 00:08:32.950
'Most people make the mistake of thinking
design is what it looks like.

00:08:32.950 --> 00:08:37.241
'People think it's this veneer -- that the
designers are handed this box and told, 'Make

00:08:37.241 --> 00:08:38.440
it look good!'

00:08:38.440 --> 00:08:40.240
That's not what we think design is.

00:08:40.240 --> 00:08:43.200
It's not just what it looks like and feels
like.

00:08:43.200 --> 00:08:44.890
Design is how it works.''

00:08:44.890 --> 00:08:51.190
He entrusted him to execute his vision of
building beautiful and functional products.

00:08:51.190 --> 00:08:57.670
Jobs told Isaacson that Ive “has more operational
power than anyone else at Apple except me.”

00:08:57.670 --> 00:09:01.329
The first order of business was to create
a new desktop.

00:09:01.329 --> 00:09:04.410
Apple had many products, but no stand-out
product.

00:09:04.410 --> 00:09:09.390
The company was close to bankruptcy due to
declining sales as it faced competition from

00:09:09.390 --> 00:09:13.820
PCs with Microsoft’s new operating system,
Windows.

00:09:13.820 --> 00:09:17.079
Engineers had played a leading role in the
design process up until then.

00:09:17.079 --> 00:09:21.400
They determined the size of the device, and
then the design team had to design an enclosure

00:09:21.400 --> 00:09:22.720
that fit.

00:09:22.720 --> 00:09:27.579
But when Jobs returned as CEO, the design
team started calling more of the shots.

00:09:27.579 --> 00:09:32.980
Ive constantly butted heads with Jon Rubinstein,
Apple's head of engineering, who was actually

00:09:32.980 --> 00:09:34.850
his supervisor.

00:09:34.850 --> 00:09:39.540
Engineers weren’t happy that they were now
forced to fit hardware into a design.

00:09:39.540 --> 00:09:43.790
While thinking about the new computer, a member
of the design team suggested it should be

00:09:43.790 --> 00:09:45.100
translucent.

00:09:45.100 --> 00:09:49.850
This was a complete contrast to the dark,
grey look of computers back then.

00:09:49.850 --> 00:09:51.060
Ive loved the idea.

00:09:51.060 --> 00:09:56.540
“A lot of people at that point in time were
nervous around computers, around technology.

00:09:56.540 --> 00:10:05.089
So, our clear goal was how we can make the
product accessible and not intimidating.”

00:10:05.089 --> 00:10:08.200
Translucency made it less intimidating.

00:10:08.200 --> 00:10:10.920
Ive also added a handle to the computer.

00:10:10.920 --> 00:10:16.130
This was not for carrying but to build a bond
with the user, to make it seem more approachable.

00:10:16.130 --> 00:10:20.040
He explained to Isaacson: “If you’re scared
of something, then you won’t touch it.

00:10:20.040 --> 00:10:22.480
I could see my mum being scared to touch it.

00:10:22.480 --> 00:10:26.790
So I thought, if there’s this handle on
it, it makes a relationship possible.”

00:10:26.790 --> 00:10:31.690
The importance of a personal connection was
reflected in the name of the computer.

00:10:31.690 --> 00:10:37.230
The “i” in iMac stood for internet but
it also stood for individual.

00:10:37.230 --> 00:10:42.639
It was a make-or-break moment for Apple when
Jobs unveiled the iMac in May 1998.

00:10:42.639 --> 00:10:45.850
The whole thing is translucent; you can see
into it.

00:10:45.850 --> 00:10:47.149
It’s so cool!

00:10:47.149 --> 00:10:50.350
Despite his enthusiasm, the reviews were mixed.

00:10:50.350 --> 00:10:55.230
Journalists noted the lack of a floppy drive
which Jobs felt would date the machine.

00:10:55.230 --> 00:10:58.070
He wanted something that represented the future.

00:10:58.070 --> 00:11:02.290
A reporter with the Boston Globe predicted
that “The iMac will only sell to some of

00:11:02.290 --> 00:11:04.079
the true believers.”

00:11:04.079 --> 00:11:06.190
How wrong he was.

00:11:06.190 --> 00:11:08.589
People went CRAZY for the iMac.

00:11:08.589 --> 00:11:14.350
It went on sale for $1,299, and by the end
of 1998, the iMac became the fastest-selling

00:11:14.350 --> 00:11:16.769
desktop in history.

00:11:16.769 --> 00:11:21.399
The iMac had a huge impact on our culture
- suddenly, it seemed like everything in the

00:11:21.399 --> 00:11:24.850
late nineties and early 2000s was transparent!

00:11:24.850 --> 00:11:29.519
The iMac was constantly upgraded and also
released in multiple colors.

00:11:29.519 --> 00:11:34.660
It set a precedent for the iPod, which was
constantly improved and released in colorful

00:11:34.660 --> 00:11:35.660
designs.

00:11:35.660 --> 00:11:36.660
There it is right there

00:11:36.660 --> 00:11:40.060
Jobs saw an opportunity to create a portable
music device that relied on the latest digital

00:11:40.060 --> 00:11:42.690
technology rather than CDs.

00:11:42.690 --> 00:11:46.720
He wanted something small enough to fit in
your pocket and easy to use.

00:11:46.720 --> 00:11:50.140
A wheel was a clever way to navigate through
the menu.

00:11:50.140 --> 00:11:52.630
Interestingly, there was no on-off button.

00:11:52.630 --> 00:11:57.209
Any button could turn the device on, and it
turned off after a period of inactivity which

00:11:57.209 --> 00:11:59.240
spoke to its ease of use.

00:11:59.240 --> 00:12:04.470
The iPod was beautiful - besides the stainless
steel back that was easily scratchable.

00:12:04.470 --> 00:12:10.000
Ive decided that the first iPod should be
white to emphasize the simplicity and elegance

00:12:10.000 --> 00:12:11.480
of the device.

00:12:11.480 --> 00:12:15.529
This was a breakthrough moment because white
would become the staple color in Apple’s

00:12:15.529 --> 00:12:18.740
lineup and is synonymous with the brand.

00:12:18.740 --> 00:12:23.550
With Apple’s success, Jony was generously
rewarded and was able to enjoy himself in

00:12:23.550 --> 00:12:26.290
his downtime and splurge a little.

00:12:26.290 --> 00:12:31.570
He had an affinity for British luxury cars
which nearly killed him.

00:12:31.570 --> 00:12:36.130
While driving with another Apple designer
in the passenger seat, Ive lost control of

00:12:36.130 --> 00:12:38.959
his Aston Martin and struck a median.

00:12:38.959 --> 00:12:43.709
The car was completely totaled, but thankfully,
both men survived.

00:12:43.709 --> 00:12:46.300
And there was still a lot to accomplish.

00:12:46.300 --> 00:12:50.389
The wheel on the iPod may have been a clever
way to navigate through the menu.

00:12:50.389 --> 00:12:53.380
But what about something even more intuitive.

00:12:53.380 --> 00:12:59.250
When the engineering team produced a multi-touch
system as a demo, Ive knew it would be a game

00:12:59.250 --> 00:13:00.250
changer.

00:13:00.250 --> 00:13:04.480
He decided to show it to Jobs privately because
his boss had a tendency to flat-out reject

00:13:04.480 --> 00:13:09.029
ideas, and Ive didn’t want him to reject
this one so quickly, especially in front of

00:13:09.029 --> 00:13:10.060
the entire team.

00:13:10.060 --> 00:13:13.339
In this case, he had nothing to worry about.

00:13:13.339 --> 00:13:15.920
Jobs agreed touch was the future.

00:13:15.920 --> 00:13:19.480
The idea of a touch device led to the iPhone.

00:13:19.480 --> 00:13:23.570
As with all Apple products, the iPhone was
built in secret.

00:13:23.570 --> 00:13:27.020
Most of Apple’s employees were not allowed
in the design lab.

00:13:27.020 --> 00:13:32.440
He showed Jobs prototypes of top-secret designs
on wooden tables outside his office, which

00:13:32.440 --> 00:13:35.850
inspired the big open tables at Apple stores.

00:13:35.850 --> 00:13:39.610
The models were covered with a black cloth
when not in use.

00:13:39.610 --> 00:13:43.370
Ive believed nothing should detract from the
iPhone screen.

00:13:43.370 --> 00:13:48.019
He wanted it to look like an infinity pool
where the display seemed to extend endlessly

00:13:48.019 --> 00:13:50.170
with no visible edges or boundaries.

00:13:50.170 --> 00:13:55.360
Today, Apple is going to reinvent the phone.

00:13:55.360 --> 00:13:56.721
Apple released the iPhone in 2007.

00:13:56.721 --> 00:13:59.720
If I want to call Jony, I’ll I do is push
his mobile number, and I’m calling him right

00:13:59.720 --> 00:14:00.720
now.

00:14:00.720 --> 00:14:01.720
Hello Steve.

00:14:01.720 --> 00:14:02.720
Hey Jony, how are you doing?

00:14:02.720 --> 00:14:07.089
Well it’s 2.5 years and I can’t tell you
how thrilled I am to make the first public

00:14:07.089 --> 00:14:11.740
phone call with iPhone.

00:14:11.740 --> 00:14:17.449
The iPhone is widely regarded as one of the
most influential product designs of the 21st

00:14:17.449 --> 00:14:18.449
century.

00:14:18.449 --> 00:14:20.880
But there is one more thing

00:14:20.880 --> 00:14:26.550
Why not make the screen bigger and turn it
into another product?

00:14:26.550 --> 00:14:30.509
And we call it the iPad

00:14:30.509 --> 00:14:38.300
Although the iPad came out after the iPhone,
Jobs said the idea for a tablet came first.

00:14:38.300 --> 00:14:43.459
Apple once again established itself as the
standard-bearer for innovative products.

00:14:43.459 --> 00:14:48.470
By the time the iPad was released in 2010,
Jobs had already been diagnosed with pancreatic

00:14:48.470 --> 00:14:49.540
cancer.

00:14:49.540 --> 00:14:53.510
Despite multiple treatments and surgeries,
his health deteriorated.

00:14:53.510 --> 00:14:59.290
A year later, in August 2011, he stepped down
as CEO.

00:14:59.290 --> 00:15:04.150
When he was recovering from his first bout
of cancer, Jobs asked to see two people: his

00:15:04.150 --> 00:15:09.790
wife Laurene and Jony Ive, as recounted in
the book by Leander Kahney.

00:15:09.790 --> 00:15:12.209
Jobs referred to Ive as his “spiritual partner.”

00:15:12.209 --> 00:15:18.300
They had worked together for nearly 15 years
and had lunch together almost every day.

00:15:18.300 --> 00:15:24.250
One month after Jobs resigned from Apple,
he died on October 5, 2011.

00:15:24.250 --> 00:15:27.720
He was only 56 years old.

00:15:27.720 --> 00:15:32.860
He famously said during a Stanford commencement
address that “death is very likely the single

00:15:32.860 --> 00:15:34.690
best invention of life.

00:15:34.690 --> 00:15:36.279
It is Life's change agent.

00:15:36.279 --> 00:15:40.769
It clears out the old to make way for the
new”.

00:15:40.769 --> 00:15:45.949
Jobs' family held a private funeral that four
Apple executives attended, including new CEO

00:15:45.949 --> 00:15:51.339
Tim Cook, formerly Apple’s chief operating
officer, and Jony Ive.

00:15:51.339 --> 00:15:55.670
On the 10th anniversary of Jobs’ death,
Jony wrote a touching letter about his friend

00:15:55.670 --> 00:15:58.209
that was published in the Wall Street Journal.

00:15:58.209 --> 00:16:00.720
“I think about Steve every day.”

00:16:00.720 --> 00:16:03.550
He said he and Steve’s widow Laurene are
close.

00:16:03.550 --> 00:16:08.480
“When her brilliant and inquisitive children
ask me about their dad I just cannot help

00:16:08.480 --> 00:16:09.480
myself.

00:16:09.480 --> 00:16:14.360
I can talk happily for hours describing the
remarkable man I loved so deeply.”

00:16:14.360 --> 00:16:16.980
“I loved how he saw the world.

00:16:16.980 --> 00:16:19.300
The way he thought was profoundly beautiful.”

00:16:19.300 --> 00:16:25.529
“He truly believed that by making something
useful, empowering and beautiful, we express

00:16:25.529 --> 00:16:28.500
our love for humanity.”

00:16:28.500 --> 00:16:32.779
Some wondered if Ive might return to the UK
after Jobs’ death.

00:16:32.779 --> 00:16:36.550
In 2012, he was knighted and became Sir Jony
Ive.

00:16:36.550 --> 00:16:42.100
But Ive remained at Apple for another two
decades and helped push for the Apple Watch.

00:16:42.100 --> 00:16:48.700
In 2019, he left Apple to start his own design
firm, LoveFrom, where Apple became his client.

00:16:48.700 --> 00:16:52.959
He had reportedly grown disillusioned with
Apple under Cook, which had begun to focus

00:16:52.959 --> 00:16:59.149
increasingly on selling software and services
rather than on sweeping design changes.

00:16:59.149 --> 00:17:03.579
Apple and Ive’s design firm parted ways
in 2022.

00:17:03.579 --> 00:17:06.230
Apple would not be Apple without Jony Ive.

00:17:06.230 --> 00:17:10.880
The question now is whether Apple can continue
to turn out beautifully crafted, innovative

